# nethersec release-age package applier: tuning.
# install.sh --with-applier writes /etc/nethersec/apply.env (root:root, mode 600) from
# this file and never overwrites an existing one. The applier reads HOSTSEC_SOURCE + the
# report transport from report.env; this file is only the HOSTSEC_APPLY_* tuning.
# docs/host/applier.md explains the design.

# Master enable. 0 = the applier releases all OUR holds and exits (fully reversible).
# Only 1 runs it; any other value counts as 0 and is logged.
#HOSTSEC_APPLY_ENABLED=1

# DRY-RUN. Ships as 1: the applier classifies, holds/unholds as normal, but only
# LOGS the set it would apply (applies nothing). Flip to 0 per box after watching one
# clean cycle in the journal (the canary step). THIS IS THE ROLLOUT SAFETY VALVE.
# Only the exact value 0 installs. Any other value (empty, false, no, a typo, a
# comment after the value) stays in dry-run and logs that it did.
HOSTSEC_APPLY_DRYRUN=1

# Scope. 0 (recommended) = keep Ubuntu -security FAST (unattended-upgrades applies
# it; the applier ignores it) and gate only -updates + third-party origins. 1 = also
# grace-gate Ubuntu -security; DISCOURAGED: it lengthens exposure to known,
# published Canonical CVE fixes for little supply-chain gain (docs/host/applier.md).
#HOSTSEC_APPLY_BLANKET=0

# Release-age grace (DAYS) for gated pockets: Ubuntu -updates + third-party origins.
# The apt analog of npm minimumReleaseAge. Default 4; 3 to 5 is a sensible range.
#HOSTSEC_APPLY_GRACE_DAYS=4

# Grace (DAYS) for Ubuntu -security; INERT unless HOSTSEC_APPLY_BLANKET=1.
#HOSTSEC_APPLY_SECUPDATE_GRACE_DAYS=0

# Grace (DAYS) for opted-in high-velocity third-party packages (see FASTPATH note).
#HOSTSEC_APPLY_CRITICAL_GATED_GRACE_DAYS=1

# Hard ceiling (DAYS). A package pending this long (episode age, survives version
# churn) but still not applied posts a "stuck" page; never a silently unpatched
# box. Must be > GRACE_DAYS with margin.
#HOSTSEC_APPLY_CEILING_DAYS=10

# apt lists older than this (HOURS) -> the clock is meaningless; apply nothing +
# signal. apt-daily.timer owns cache freshness; the applier never runs apt-get update.
#HOSTSEC_APPLY_STALECACHE_HOURS=48

# Re-assert a still-standing card at most this often (SECONDS). Default 6h.
#HOSTSEC_APPLY_RESURFACE_SECS=21600

# Post at most this many cards per run (backlog flood guard).
#HOSTSEC_APPLY_MAX_POSTS=8

# apt's own runs. Each run first waits (up to this many SECONDS) while apt-daily.service
# or unattended-upgrades' install is running, then stands down if apt is still busy.
#HOSTSEC_APPLY_APT_BUSY_WAIT_SECS=600
#HOSTSEC_APPLY_APT_BUSY_POLL_SECS=5
# A simulation or apply that fails while apt was busy (or after apt-get update swapped
# the lists in mid-run) is retried next run without a card. The Nth such run in a row
# pages as before, so a real failure stays hidden for at most N-1 runs. A run whose
# lists changed after it sorted the set never installs, the Nth one included (that one
# pages apply-apt-collisions instead).
#HOSTSEC_APPLY_COLLIDE_PAGE_RUNS=3

# Crown-jewel package NAMES (space-separated globs) fast-tracked at age 0; but ONLY
# from a CANONICAL origin (see TRUSTED_ORIGINS below). A signature alone is not enough:
# a crown-jewel NAME from any other origin, a signed third-party repo included, is NOT
# fast-pathed; it soaks for ANOM_GRACE_DAYS AND is carded as an anomaly. A superset is
# safe (a name that isn't installed is a no-op). Setting this REPLACES the built-in
# list, so copy the defaults from the script and add box-specifics to them, e.g. a mail
# server's 'postfix dovecot* opendkim'. The default already includes
# libwebp/libtiff/libjpeg/libpng for image-handling boxes.
#HOSTSEC_APPLY_FASTPATH_PKGS="openssh-server libc6 sudo openssl libssl3 ... (see script default)"

# What counts as CANONICAL (Ubuntu, GPG-signed BY CANONICAL); only canonical
# packages take the fast-path or count as real -security. apt's "trusted" bit is TRUE
# for ANY signed repo (Docker CE, PPAs, NodeSource), so trust alone is NOT enough. A
# package is canonical iff it is signed AND its origin label is in TRUSTED_ORIGINS AND
# its fetch site ends with one of TRUSTED_SITES. The default trusts Ubuntu's own
# mirrors (everything under .ubuntu.com). If a box pulls Ubuntu from another mirror (a
# cloud provider's, say), ADD that host here, keeping .ubuntu.com, or its legit updates
# will be gated (safe, slower). The packages must still carry Canonical's signature.
#HOSTSEC_APPLY_TRUSTED_ORIGINS="Ubuntu UbuntuESM UbuntuESMApps"
#HOSTSEC_APPLY_TRUSTED_SITES=".ubuntu.com mirror.example.com"

# Soak (DAYS) for a crown-jewel NAME arriving from a NON-canonical origin; the most
# suspicious class (a third-party repo shadowing e.g. libssl3). Defaults to
# GRACE_DAYS; keep it >= GRACE_DAYS (never let the riskiest class age in fastest).
#HOSTSEC_APPLY_ANOM_GRACE_DAYS=4

# Package names that get a SHORTER soak than GRACE_DAYS, meant for third-party
# (non-Canonical) packages; never age 0, which would be a hole for an untrusted
# origin. Space-separated globs. Empty by default.
# Use for high-velocity critical surfaces you accept a 1-day soak on, e.g.:
#HOSTSEC_APPLY_CRITICAL_GATED_PKGS="docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io runc nodejs"

# Kernel packages the guarded apply may NEWLY install without soaking (space-separated
# globs). A kernel meta bump (linux-image-virtual / -generic) installs the versioned
# binaries of the new kernel; any OTHER new install refuses the whole apply set. Every
# entry is an age-0 exemption, so keep it narrow. This REPLACES the default (include
# the defaults when adding); empty = the default below. Add e.g. 'linux-tools-*' only
# on a box whose linux-tools-generic meta actually pulls it in (the card names it).
#HOSTSEC_APPLY_KERNEL_NEWINSTALL_GLOBS="linux-image-* linux-headers-* linux-modules-* linux-modules-extra-* linux-main-modules-*"

# Deny-list file: one 'pkg' or 'pkg=version' per line (# comments ok). A
# matching upgrade is held INDEFINITELY + carded; the lever to stop a disclosed-bad
# version now. (The kill switch is NOT that lever: it releases all holds.)
#HOSTSEC_APPLY_DENYLIST_FILE=/etc/nethersec/apply-denylist

# Auto-reboot after a kernel/libc apply. UNUSED: reboot stays manual and
# detector-alerted. Kept for a later opt-in.
#HOSTSEC_APPLY_AUTOREBOOT=0
